╭─ VULN-0001 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Pre-authentication heap buffer overflow via unbounded appends to     │
│  the per-client MQTT write buffer                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 8.2                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: sol MQTT broker (C, v0.18.5) - src/                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: MQTT SUBSCRIBE handler (src/handlers.c subscribe_handler, memcpy  │
│  at line 617)                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: MQTT SUBSCRIBE (raw TCP)                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The broker allocates each client's write buffer at exactly                  │
│  `max_request_size` bytes but no code path ever checks whether the next      │
│  packet fits, so protocol messages larger than the remaining space are       │
│  written past the end of the heap allocation. A single unauthenticated       │
│  SUBSCRIBE that replays retained messages - or a session resume, or a QoS    │
│  retransmit - overruns the buffer with bytes the attacker chose, and the     │
│  shipped configuration crashes. The same unchecked append exists at twelve   │
│  further write sites, so the buffer can also be overrun by ordinary          │
│  protocol traffic once it is nearly full.                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An unauthenticated network peer can write attacker-chosen bytes of          │
│  attacker-chosen length beyond a heap allocation inside the broker process,  │
│  and reliably terminates the service. Demonstrated consequences are heap     │
│  corruption outside the `wbuf` object (the overflowed bytes were proven to   │
│  be the attacker's own PUBLISH payload, landing at `wbuf+908` in a           │
│  1024-byte buffer) and process death: AddressSanitizer aborts, and the       │
│  plain optimized build exits with SIGSEGV on the same input. Because the     │
│  broker is the single broker in the deployment, every connected client       │
│  loses its session and message flow, giving a trivial denial of service      │
│  against all tenants. The write primitive also reaches adjacent heap         │
│  objects and `write_data()` will transmit `towrite - wrote` bytes from       │
│  `wbuf`, so out-of-bounds read/disclosure of neighbouring heap contents is   │
│  reachable once the length tracker is corrupted; that read-back and          │
│  control-flow hijack were not demonstrated and are not scored here.          │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  `client_init()` sizes the write buffer with exactly                         │
│  `conf->max_request_size` bytes and only ever resets the write cursor:       │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/server.c startLineNumber=395 endLineNumber=399               │
│      client->wrote   = ATOMIC_VAR_INIT(0);                                   │
│      client->towrite = ATOMIC_VAR_INIT(0);                                   │
│      if (!client->wbuf)                                                      │
│          client->wbuf =                                                      │
│              try_calloc(conf->max_request_size, sizeof(unsigned char));      │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  No write site compares `c->towrite + len` against that size. The            │
│  highest-impact sink is the retained-message replay in `subscribe_handler`,  │
│  which copies a stored, attacker-created PUBLISH into the buffer once per    │
│  decoded SUBSCRIBE tuple:                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/handlers.c startLineNumber=613 endLineNumber=619             │
│          // Retained message? Publish it                                     │
│          // TODO move after SUBACK response                                  │
│          if (t->retained_msg) {                                              │
│              size_t len = alloc_size(t->retained_msg);                       │
│              memcpy(c->wbuf + c->towrite, t->retained_msg, len);             │
│              c->towrite += len;                                              │
│          }                                                                   │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `len` comes from `alloc_size(t->retained_msg)`, i.e. the size of a          │
│  serialized PUBLISH that the attacker produced via `publish_handler`         │
│  (`t->retained_msg = try_alloc(mqtt_size(&e->data, NULL));                   │
│  mqtt_pack(&e->data, t->retained_msg);`), and `topic_store_get_or_put`       │
│  returns the same `struct topic *` for a repeated topic name, so duplicate   │
│  tuples replay the same blob repeatedly. The only bound applied to inbound   │
│  packets is `pktlen <= max_request_size` in `recv_packet`, which constrains  │
│  a single PUBLISH, not the sum of the replays.                               │
│                                                                              │
│  The same unchecked pattern appears at every other producer of outbound      │
│  bytes, which is why the defect is a property of the buffer contract rather  │
│  than of one handler. `set_connack()` even carried the missing check as a    │
│  TODO:                                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/handlers.c startLineNumber=330 endLineNumber=338             │
│          // TODO check for write buffer size exceed                          │
│          if (list_size(c->session->outgoing_msgs) > 0) {                     │
│              size_t len = 0;                                                 │
│              list_foreach(item, c->session->outgoing_msgs)                   │
│              {                                                               │
│                  len = mqtt_size(item->data, NULL);                          │
│                  mqtt_pack(item->data, c->wbuf + c->towrite);                │
│                  c->towrite += len;                                          │
│              }                                                               │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  and the inflight retransmit timer repacks every unacknowledged message and  │
│  every pending ack in one pass:                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/server.c startLineNumber=336 endLineNumber=339               │
│                  size = mqtt_size(c->session->i_msgs[i].packet, NULL);       │
│                  // Serialize the packet and send it out again               │
│                  mqtt_pack(p, c->wbuf + c->towrite);                         │
│                  c->towrite += size;                                         │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  The ACK branch of that timer (line 354) additionally sized `&ack` but       │
│  packed `p`, so the reservation and the write disagreed even when a bound    │
│  was applied - that mismatch is fixed as part of the same site.              │
│                                                                              │
│  Finally, the corrupted `towrite` is consumed by the writer without any      │
│  clamp, so the effect does not stop at the memcpy: `send_data(&c->conn,      │
│  c->wbuf + c->wrote, c->towrite - c->wrote);` transmits past the             │
│  allocation. `git blame` on the retained-replay lines attributes them to     │
│  the bulk import commit that introduced the broker (`6c4db6f`, "Import       │
│  code"), i.e. this is original code, not a regression.                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Three sinks were reproduced end to end, all pre-authentication under the    │
│  default `allow_anonymous true` posture, and all sharing the single root     │
│  cause: no producer ever establishes `towrite + len <= max_request_size`     │
│  before writing.                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Build the broker with AddressSanitizer and start it on 127.0.0.1 with    │
│  `max_request_size 1024`, `allow_anonymous true`.                            │
│  2. Open a first connection and publish two retained PUBLISH packets         │
│  (`a/000`, `a/001`) with a 900-byte payload; each stored retained blob       │
│  serializes to 910 bytes.                                                    │
│  3. Open a second connection and send one SUBSCRIBE packet carrying both     │
│  topic tuples at QoS 0.                                                      │
│  4. The replay loop copies 910 bytes at offset 0 and then 910 bytes at       │
│  offset 910 into a 1024-byte heap buffer: AddressSanitizer reports a         │
│  `heap-buffer-overflow` WRITE of size 910 immediately after the 1032-byte    │
│  region (1024 bytes plus the allocator header) at `src/handlers.c:617`, and  │
│  the process aborts.                                                         │
│  5. Repeat with one tuple only (910 bytes, fits): the broker stays alive     │
│  and no sanitizer report is produced, confirming the trigger is the          │
│  unbounded append rather than the retained-message feature.                  │
│  6. Repeat against the plain optimized build with six tuples: the broker     │
│  exits with SIGSEGV (status -11) instead of an ASan abort, confirming a      │
│  production-build crash.                                                     │
│  7. Scale the same sequence to `max_request_size` 1 MB (400 retained         │
│  messages of 3000 bytes, 400 tuples) and to the shipped 50 MB (52 retained   │
│  messages of 1 MiB) to confirm the defect is not an artefact of the small    │
│  test configuration.                                                         │
│                                                                              │
