│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Introduce a single bounded append primitive for the per-client write        │
│  buffer and route every producer of outbound bytes through it. The helper    │
│  must compare the requested length against both the configured buffer size   │
│  and the bytes already pending, advance the write cursor only when the       │
│  record fits, and otherwise drop the record and log the event so no caller   │
│  can write past the allocation. The comparison has to be overflow-safe,      │
│  which means testing `len > limit - used` rather than `used + len > limit`,  │
│  and it must also handle a cursor that is already beyond the limit. Apply    │
│  the helper at every site that serialises into the buffer, not only at the   │
│  retained-message replay: retained replay, session-resume flush, publish     │
│  fan-out, the inflight retransmit of messages and of acks, and the           │
│  fixed-size CONNACK, SUBACK, UNSUBACK, PUBACK/PUBREC, PUBREL, PUBCOMP and    │
│  PINGRESP appends. The small fixed-size appends matter because a buffer      │
│  filled to within a few bytes by a large record is overrun by the            │
│  acknowledgement that follows it. Because the same buffer is drained by the  │
│  socket writer using the pending-bytes counter, the helper is also what      │
│  keeps that send within bounds; no separate clamp is needed once no site     │
│  can advance the cursor past the limit. The inflight ACK retransmit          │
│  additionally has to pack the packet whose size was computed, rather than    │
│  an unrelated packet, otherwise a correctly sized reservation is still       │
│  overrun by a larger write. Finally, build the acknowledgement header from   │
│  the recorded QoS instead of leaving the packet header uninitialised, so     │
│  the retransmit serialises a well-defined packet. Consider raising           │
│  `max_request_size` awareness in the protocol layer - for example refusing   │
│  to replay more retained data than can fit in one write cycle and resuming   │
│  later - so that a legitimate large subscription degrades gracefully         │
│  instead of silently dropping replies.                                       │
│                                                                              │
