╭─ VULN-0002 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Pre-authentication use-after-free of a shared MQTT session on        │
│  ClientId reuse                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: MEDIUM                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 5.3                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: sol MQTT broker (C, v0.18.5) - src/                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: MQTT CONNECT handler (src/handlers.c connect_handler, session     │
│  lookup at lines 400-405)                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The handler that lets a second connection resume an existing MQTT session   │
│  by ClientId does not take a reference on that session, while the            │
│  connection that created it releases the only reference when it              │
│  disconnects. The session object is therefore freed while the second         │
│  connection is still connected and still using it, and that connection then  │
│  reads and writes freed heap memory. Any unauthenticated peer can set this   │
│  up with two connections and one disconnect.                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  A second unauthenticated connection can obtain a ClientId's session, be     │
│  told by the broker that it resumed that session (`session_present=1`), and  │
│  keep using a session object that the broker frees as soon as the first      │
│  connection disconnects. The demonstrated consequence is that the surviving  │
│  connection then operates on freed heap memory: it copies 64 bytes of freed  │
│  session data into a new subscriber record and links that record into a      │
│  subscription list that teardown already destroyed. Under AddressSanitizer   │
│  this aborts the connection thread; on the plain optimized build it          │
│  completes silently, leaving the connection reading and writing a 328-byte   │
│  heap block that is now reusable by any other allocation in the process.     │
│  Demonstrated integrity impact is limited to that corruption of freed        │
│  memory, and no service interruption was observed on the production build;   │
│  what would turn this into code execution is reclaiming the freed block      │
│  with attacker-controlled bytes, which was attempted but not achieved        │
│  because the two connections are served by different event-loop threads and  │
│  the chunk was not handed back within the test window.                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  Session lifetime is reference counted (`struct client_session` embeds a     │
│  `struct ref`, `src/handlers.c:111`), but the reference count does not       │
│  model the number of connections that share a session. `connect_handler`     │
│  takes the only reference when it creates a session, and the reuse branch    │
│  assigns the same pointer without taking one:                                │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/handlers.c startLineNumber=399 endLineNumber=420             │
│      // First we check if a session is present                               │
│      HASH_FIND_STR(server.sessions, cc->client_id, cc->session);             │
│      if (cc->session && c->bits.clean_session == true)                       │
│          // Clean session true, we have to clean old session, if any         │
│          HASH_DEL(server.sessions, cc->session);                             │
│      else if (cc->session)                                                   │
│          session_present = 1;                                                │
│      ...                                                                     │
│      if (c->bits.clean_session == true || !cc->session) {                    │
│          cc->session = client_session_alloc(cc->client_id);                  │
│          INCREF(cc->session, struct client_session);                         │
│          HASH_ADD_STR(server.sessions, session_id, cc->session);             │
│      }                                                                       │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Line 405 is the defect: the resuming connection is told it owns the         │
│  session (`session_present = 1`, CONNACK `0x01`) and `cc->session` points    │
│  at it, yet the count stays at one. The single reference is then released    │
│  by whichever connection created it, in the clean-session branch of          │
│  teardown:                                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/server.c startLineNumber=430 endLineNumber=438               │
│      if (client->clean_session == true) {                                    │
│          if (client->session) {                                              │
│              topic_store_remove_wildcard(server.store, client->client_id);   │
│              list_foreach(item, client->session->subscriptions)              │
│              {                                                               │
│                  topic_del_subscriber(item->data, client);                   │
│              }                                                               │
│              HASH_DEL(server.sessions, client->session);                     │
│              DECREF(client->session, struct client_session);                 │
│          }                                                                   │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `DECREF` drives the count to zero, `session_free` destroys both lists and   │
│  frees the block (`src/handlers.c:89-98`), and the second connection keeps   │
│  a dangling pointer. Every handler that touches `e->client->session` then    │
│  operates on freed memory; the runtime trigger lands in the SUBSCRIBE path,  │
│  where `subscriber_new` copies the freed `session_id` and `list_push` links  │
│  into the destroyed `subscriptions` list:                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/handlers.c startLineNumber=588 endLineNumber=593             │
│          struct subscriber *sub = subscriber_new(e->client->session,         │
│  s->tuples[i].qos);                                                          │
│          ...                                                                 │
│                      list_push(e->client->session->subscriptions, t);        │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Two further consequences of the same missing ownership model are visible    │
│  in the surrounding code. The `clean_session == true` discard at line 403    │
│  removes the map entry without releasing its reference, so repeatedly        │
│  connecting with an existing ClientId and `clean_session=1` abandons a ~1.5  │
│  MB session allocation each time. And teardown releases the reference only   │
│  for clean-session clients, so a persistent client that resumed a session    │
│  never releases anything, which is the mirror image of the same asymmetry.   │
│  Fixing the reference count requires stating the model explicitly - the map  │
│  owns one reference while registered, each connection owns one while it      │
│  holds the pointer - and then making creation, reuse and teardown agree      │
│  with it.                                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  The `git blame` for lines 400-419 attributes the session lookup and         │
│  creation logic to the same bulk import commit that introduced the broker    │
│  (`6c4db6f`), so this is original logic rather than a regression introduced  │
│  by a later change.                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Start the broker with anonymous access enabled and a known               │
│  `max_request_size`.                                                         │
│  2. Connection A sends CONNECT with ClientId `victim` and                    │
│  `clean_session=1`. The broker creates session S and takes the only          │
│  reference to it; CONNACK reports `session_present=0`.                       │
│  3. Connection B sends CONNECT with the same ClientId `victim` and           │
│  `clean_session=0`. The broker finds S, sets `session_present=1` in the      │
│  CONNACK and assigns `B->session = S` without taking a reference.            │
│  4. Close A's socket. The teardown path takes the `clean_session == true`    │
│  branch, removes S from the sessions map, decrements the reference count     │
│  from 1 to 0 and frees S - while B is still connected and still points at    │
│  it.                                                                         │
│  5. B sends SUBSCRIBE. The handler reaches                                   │
│  `subscriber_new(e->client->session, ...)`, which copies 64 bytes from the   │
│  freed session (`src/subscriber.c:45`), and then                             │
│  `list_push(e->client->session->subscriptions, t)` (`src/handlers.c:593`),   │
│  which writes into the subscription list that `session_free` destroyed.      │
│  6. Under AddressSanitizer the run reports `heap-use-after-free` READ of     │
│  size 64 with the free attributed to `session_free` via                      │
│  `client_deactivate`, and the broker aborts. On the plain optimized build    │
│  the same sequence completes without a signal, leaving the corruption        │
│  silent.                                                                     │
│  7. Repeat the sequence against a patched build: B receives a valid SUBACK   │
│  for its packet identifier and no sanitizer report is produced.              │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python                                                                   │
│  #!/usr/bin/env python3                                                      │
│  """Heap-use-after-free: an MQTT session reused for a second ClientId        │
│  connection                                                                  │
│  without taking a reference, then freed by the original clean_session        │
│  client.                                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  connect_handler:                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│      HASH_FIND_STR(server.sessions, cc->client_id, cc->session);             │
│      if (cc->session && c->bits.clean_session == true)                       │
│          HASH_DEL(server.sessions, cc->session);                             │
│      else if (cc->session)                                                   │
│          session_present = 1;                     /* <-- no INCREF taken */  │
│      ...                                                                     │
│      if (c->bits.clean_session == true || !cc->session) {                    │
│          cc->session = client_session_alloc(cc->client_id);                  │
│          INCREF(cc->session, struct client_session);   /* only on create */  │
│          HASH_ADD_STR(server.sessions, session_id, cc->session);             │
│      }                                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  client_deactivate() frees the session when the owning clean_session client  │
│  disconnects, leaving the second client with a dangling session pointer      │
│  that                                                                        │
│  subscribe_handler dereferences.                                             │
│  """                                                                         │
