│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Give each connection its own reference to the session it uses, and make     │
│  teardown release exactly the references that were taken, so that no         │
│  connection can be left holding a block whose count has reached zero.        │
│  Establish an explicit ownership model first: the sessions map holds one     │
│  reference while the session is registered, and every connection that        │
│  points at the session holds one of its own. Under that model the creation   │
│  path must take two references, the reuse path must take one for the         │
│  resuming connection, and the clean-session discard path must release the    │
│  map reference while clearing the local pointer. Teardown must then release  │
│  the connection's reference on every path, including connections whose       │
│  clean-session flag is false, and release the map reference only when the    │
│  session is being discarded. Setting the client's session pointer to null    │
│  as part of that release prevents a recycled client block from releasing a   │
│  stale reference. Apply the same symmetry to the branch that discards an     │
│  existing session for a clean-session connection, which currently removes    │
│  the map entry without releasing its reference and therefore leaks the       │
│  block on every repeat. Beyond the reference counting, consider binding      │
│  session ownership to the connection that created it - for example refusing  │
│  to hand an existing session to a second concurrent connection, or           │
│  explicitly closing the previous connection when a ClientId is reused - so   │
│  that two live connections cannot share mutable session state at all; MQTT   │
│  permits a connection to take over a ClientId, but the broker should then    │
│  invalidate the previous connection rather than leave it with a pointer      │
│  into shared state. Finally, the session's subscription lists are destroyed  │
│  during teardown while other handlers can still reach them; making the       │
│  session's lifetime solely reference-counted, as above, is what removes      │
│  that window.                                                                │
│                                                                              │
