╭─ VULN-0003 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Pre-auth remote process kill via NULL dispatch on unimplemented      │
│  MQTT packet types (`handlers[]` / `unpack_handlers[]`)                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: sol MQTT v3.1.1 broker (C source snapshot, `src/`)                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: MQTT TCP listener — packet framing `recv_packet`, dispatch        │
│  `process_message` / `mqtt_unpack`                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: MQTT (raw TCP)                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  Any unauthenticated TCP peer can terminate the `sol` broker with a single   │
│  two-byte MQTT packet. The broker's fixed-header opcode gate admits every    │
│  type from CONNECT (1) to DISCONNECT (14), but the command dispatch table    │
│  has NULL entries for the packet types it does not implement, and the        │
│  dispatch entry point calls the table slot without a NULL check. Sending     │
│  PINGRESP (`D0 00`) therefore performs an indirect call to address 0. A      │
│  parallel defect exists in the decoder table, which has only 11 entries, so  │
│  UNSUBACK (type 11) indexes out of bounds while CONNACK (2) and SUBACK (9)   │
│  call NULL unpack handlers.                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  **Unauthenticated, zero-interaction availability loss for the whole broker  │
│  process.** A single two-byte packet sent on a fresh TCP connection — no     │
│  CONNECT, no credentials, no prior state — terminates the listening daemon   │
│  (SIGSEGV). Every connected client is dropped, in-flight QoS state and the   │
│  in-memory retained-message store are lost, and the broker stays down until  │
│  something outside the process restarts it. The same death is reachable      │
│  from four distinct packet types and through both dispatch tables, so        │
│  filtering a single opcode does not mitigate it.                             │
│                                                                              │
│  No confidentiality or integrity impact is claimed: the crash address is     │
│  exactly `0x0` and the call target is not attacker-controlled, so no         │
│  read/write primitive or control-flow influence was demonstrated. The        │
│  demonstrated effect is process termination only (`C:N` / `I:N` / `A:H`).    │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  ## Attacker-reachable path                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  `recv_packet` admits any opcode in the CONNECT..DISCONNECT range and never  │
│  checks whether the broker implements that packet type as an incoming        │
│  packet:                                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/server.c startLineNumber=505 endLineNumber=523               │
│          if (c->read == 2) {                                                 │
│              opcode = *c->rbuf >> 4;                                         │
│                                                                              │
│              /*                                                              │
│               * Check for OPCODE, if an unknown OPCODE is received return    │
│  an                                                                          │
│               * error                                                        │
│               */                                                             │
│              if (DISCONNECT < opcode || CONNECT > opcode)                    │
│                  return -ERRPACKETERR;                                       │
│                                                                              │
│              /*                                                              │
│               * We have a PINGRESP/PINGREQ or a DISCONNECT packet, we're     │
│  done                                                                        │
│               * here                                                         │
│               */                                                             │
│              if (opcode > UNSUBSCRIBE) {                                     │
│                  c->rpos   = 2;                                              │
│                  c->toread = c->read;                                        │
│                  goto exit;                                                  │
│              }                                                               │
│          }                                                                   │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Two of the four types of interest (PINGRESP = 13, UNSUBACK = 11) are        │
│  additionally classified as "no payload" packets, so the frame is accepted   │
│  after only two bytes are read, with `read == toread == 2`.                  │
│                                                                              │
│  `process_message` then decodes and dispatches with no regard for whether    │
│  the type is legal from a client:                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/server.c startLineNumber=858 endLineNumber=867               │
│  static void process_message(struct ev_ctx *ctx, struct client *c)           │
│  {                                                                           │
│      struct io_event io = {.client = c};                                     │
│      /*                                                                      │
│       * Unpack received bytes into a mqtt_packet structure and execute the   │
│       * correct handler based on the type of the operation.                  │
│       */                                                                     │
│      mqtt_unpack(c->rbuf + c->rpos, &io.data, *c->rbuf, c->read - c->rpos);  │
│      c->toread = c->read = c->rpos = 0;                                      │
│      c->rc = handle_command(io.data.header.bits.type, &io);                  │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Root cause 1 — unchecked command dispatch table                          │
│                                                                              │
│  The command handler table is declared with 15 entries and NULL              │
│  placeholders for CONNACK (2), SUBACK (9), UNSUBACK (11) and PINGRESP (13):  │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/handlers.c startLineNumber=61 endLineNumber=76               │
│  /* Command handler mapped usign their position paired with their type */    │
│  static handler *handlers[15] = {NULL,                                       │
│                                  connect_handler,                            │
│                                  NULL,                                       │
│                                  publish_handler,                            │
│                                  ...                                         │
│                                  NULL,                                       │
│                                  disconnect_handler};                        │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  and the only public dispatch entry point indexes it and calls the result:   │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/handlers.c startLineNumber=877 endLineNumber=880             │
│  int handle_command(unsigned type, struct io_event *event)                   │
│  {                                                                           │
│      return handlers[type](event);                                           │
│  }                                                                           │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  There is no range check (the opcode is a 4-bit field, so `type == 15`       │
│  would read one element past a 15-entry table) and no NULL check. `D0 00`    │
│  consequently performs `call *handlers[13]` with a NULL target: the          │
│  faulting instruction pointer is exactly `0x0` and `rax` holds the selected  │
│  index, `0xd`.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Root cause 2 — out-of-bounds decoder dispatch table                      │
│                                                                              │
│  The decoder table has only eleven entries, ending at UNSUBSCRIBE (10):      │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/mqtt.c startLineNumber=68 endLineNumber=82                   │
│  /*                                                                          │
│   * Unpack functions mapping unpacking_handlers positioned in the array      │
│  based                                                                       │
│   * on message type                                                          │
│   */                                                                         │
│  static mqtt_unpack_handler *unpack_handlers[11] = {NULL,                    │
│                                                     unpack_mqtt_connect,     │
│                                                     NULL,                    │
│                                                     unpack_mqtt_publish,     │
│                                                     unpack_mqtt_ack,         │
│                                                     ...                      │
│                                                     unpack_mqtt_unsubscribe  │
│  };                                                                          │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `mqtt_unpack` only short-circuits types 12..14, so UNSUBACK (11) is used    │
│  as an index into that 11-element array — an out-of-bounds read of a         │
│  function pointer that is then called — while CONNACK (2) and SUBACK (9)     │
│  call the NULL slots:                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/mqtt.c startLineNumber=422 endLineNumber=437                 │
│  int mqtt_unpack(u8 *buf, struct mqtt_packet *pkt, u8 byte, usize len)       │
│  {                                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│      int rc      = MQTT_OK;                                                  │
│      u8 type     = byte >> 4;                                                │
│                                                                              │
│      pkt->header = (union mqtt_header){.byte = byte};                        │
│                                                                              │
│      /* Call the appropriate unpack handler based on the message type */     │
│      if (type >= PINGREQ && type <= DISCONNECT)                              │
│          return rc;                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│      rc = unpack_handlers[type](buf, pkt, len);                              │
│                                                                              │
│      return rc;                                                              │
│  }                                                                           │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Reachability and preconditions                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  None beyond the ability to open a TCP connection. Decoding and dispatch     │
│  both run inside `process_message` before `connect_handler` evaluates        │
│  `allow_anonymous` or any credential, so the flaw is reachable with          │
│  `allow_anonymous false` and no CONNECT at all (verified). The packet also   │
│  works after a successful CONNECT, so authenticating does not protect        │
│  against it.                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  The two dispatch layers have to be fixed together: rejecting only type 11   │
│  leaves types 2, 9 and 13 live, and fixing only the decoder leaves the       │
│  `handlers[]` holes reachable through the types the decoder does not unpack  │
│  (the PINGREQ/DISCONNECT paths and, for type 13, the short-packet path).     │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Start the `sol` broker on a loopback port from the ASan/UBSan build      │
│  (`build-asan/sol`) or the plain optimized build (`build-dev/sol`).          │
│  2. Open a TCP connection to the listener and send the two bytes `D0 00`     │
│  (fixed header: type 13 PINGRESP, flags 0; Remaining Length 0). Do not send  │
│  a CONNECT first.                                                            │
│  3. Observe the connection close without a reply.                            │
│  4. Observe the broker terminate: AddressSanitizer reports `SEGV on unknown  │
│  address 0x000000000000 (pc 0x000000000000)` with the top frame in           │
│  `process_message` (`src/server.c:867`); the non-instrumented build dies     │
│  with signal 11.                                                             │
│  5. Repeat with `B0 00` (UNSUBACK, type 11) to reach the out-of-bounds       │
│  decoder table entry, and with `90 00 00 00` (SUBACK, type 9) and `20 00 00  │
│  00` (CONNACK, type 2) to reach NULL decoder slots; each terminates the      │
│  broker.                                                                     │
│  6. Control: repeat with `C0 00` (PINGREQ, type 12) — the broker answers     │
│  `D0 00` and keeps serving.                                                  │
│  7. Repeat step 2 against a broker started with `allow_anonymous false`      │
│  without supplying any credentials, to confirm the packet is processed       │
│  before authentication.                                                      │
