╭─ VULN-0004 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Pre-auth process kill: PUBACK/PUBCOMP for an unallocated packet id   │
│  DECREFs a NULL in-flight packet                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: sol MQTT v3.1.1 broker (C source snapshot, `src/`)                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: MQTT TCP listener — PUBACK (0x40) / PUBCOMP (0x70) handlers,      │
│  `inflight_msg_clear` / `DECREF`                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: MQTT (raw TCP)                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  A four-byte PUBACK or PUBCOMP naming a packet identifier the broker never   │
│  allocated terminates the `sol` broker. The ACK handlers take the            │
│  identifier straight from the wire, index the per-session in-flight table    │
│  and decrement the reference count of the packet stored there without        │
│  checking that the session exists or that the slot holds a live packet; for  │
│  any unallocated identifier that slot is NULL, so the reference-count write  │
│  faults. No CONNECT, credentials or prior state are required, and            │
│  re-sending a PUBACK for an identifier that was already acknowledged —       │
│  ordinary QoS 1 client behaviour — triggers the same crash.                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  **Unauthenticated, zero-interaction availability loss for the whole broker  │
│  process.** Four bytes (`40 02 00 01` or `70 02 00 01`) on a fresh TCP       │
│  connection — no CONNECT, no credentials — terminate the listener with       │
│  SIGSEGV. Every connected client is dropped and all in-memory session,       │
│  in-flight and retained-message state is lost until an external supervisor   │
│  restarts the process.                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  The same crash is also reachable **without any attacker intent**: after a   │
│  legitimate QoS 1 acknowledgement, a client that retransmits that PUBACK     │
│  (normal retry behaviour after a reconnect or timeout) dereferences the      │
│  NULL slot the first ACK just cleared, so ordinary client traffic can take   │
│  the broker down. Because a single broker process serves all clients, this   │
│  is a denial of service against the whole deployment, not just one session.  │
│                                                                              │
│  No confidentiality or integrity impact is claimed: the faulting pointer is  │
│  a fixed NULL-derived address, so no attacker-controlled write or read was   │
│  demonstrated (`C:N` / `I:N` / `A:H`).                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  ## Root cause                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  `puback_handler` and `pubcomp_handler` take the 16-bit packet identifier    │
│  straight out of the decoded packet, index the per-session in-flight table   │
│  with it, and hand the resulting slot to `inflight_msg_clear()`:             │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/handlers.c startLineNumber=785 endLineNumber=801             │
│  static int puback_handler(struct io_event *e)                               │
│  {                                                                           │
│      struct client *c = e->client;                                           │
│      unsigned pkt_id  = e->data.ack.pkt_id;                                  │
│      log_debug("Received PUBACK from %s (m%u)", c->client_id, pkt_id);       │
│  #if THREADSNR > 0                                                           │
│      pthread_mutex_lock(&c->mutex);                                          │
│  #endif                                                                      │
│      inflight_msg_clear(&c->session->i_msgs[pkt_id]);                        │
│      c->session->i_msgs[pkt_id].packet = NULL;                               │
│      c->session->i_acks[pkt_id]        = -1;                                 │
│      --c->session->inflights;                                                │
│  #if THREADSNR > 0                                                           │
│      pthread_mutex_unlock(&c->mutex);                                        │
│  #endif                                                                      │
│      return NOREPLY;                                                         │
│  }                                                                           │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  (`pubcomp_handler`, `src/handlers.c:839-855`, is the same shape with the    │
│  `i_acks` write first.)                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  `inflight_msg_clear` is a bare reference-count decrement with no NULL       │
│  check:                                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/sol_internal.h startLineNumber=389 endLineNumber=389         │
│  #define inflight_msg_clear(msg) DECREF((msg)->packet, struct mqtt_packet)   │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/ref.h startLineNumber=50 endLineNumber=54                    │
│  static inline void ref_dec(const struct ref *ref)                           │
│  {                                                                           │
│      if (--((struct ref *)ref)->count == 0)                                  │
│          ref->free(ref);                                                     │
│  }                                                                           │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Two facts turn that into a remote crash:                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  - **`c->session` can be NULL.** `client_init` sets `client->session =       │
│  NULL` (`src/server.c:402`) and nothing on the dispatch path checks          │
│  `c->connected` before running a handler, so an ACK sent as the very first   │
│  packet dereferences `NULL + offsetof(i_msgs)`.                              │
│  - **`i_msgs` is zero-filled.** `session_init` allocates it with             │
│  `try_calloc(MAX_INFLIGHT_MSGS, sizeof(struct inflight_msg))`                │
│  (`src/handlers.c:109-110`), so `i_msgs[id].packet == NULL` for every        │
│  identifier the broker never allocated. `DECREF(NULL, struct mqtt_packet)`   │
│  becomes `ref_dec` on a pointer derived from NULL, and the `--count`         │
│  read-modify-write faults near address zero.                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Packet identifiers are entirely client-chosen and the broker is not         │
│  required to have allocated any given one, so this is reachable with `40 02  │
│  00 01` (PUBACK, id 1) or `70 02 00 01` (PUBCOMP, id 1). `0xFFFF` crashes    │
│  too, confirming the index is in range and the defect is the unguarded NULL  │
│  rather than an out-of-bounds access.                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Why this also happens without an attacker                                │
│                                                                              │
│  After a legitimate PUBACK clears `i_msgs[mid].packet` and sets it to NULL,  │
│  a client that retransmits the same PUBACK — normal QoS 1 behaviour after a  │
│  reconnect or a timeout — runs the same code again with the slot now empty   │
│  and kills the broker. The broker therefore dies on its own intended         │
│  traffic pattern, not only on crafted input.                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Related defect examined and ruled out                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  The `--c->session->inflights` decrement on an `atomic_ushort` is unguarded  │
│  and would wrap to 65535, permanently satisfying `has_inflight()`. It is     │
│  **not** independently reachable: both decrement sites are preceded by       │
│  `inflight_msg_clear`, which crashes first when the slot is empty, and when  │
│  the slot is non-NULL the only writer (`publish_message`,                    │
│  `src/handlers.c:216-218`) increments the counter for the same identifier,   │
│  so the counter is at least 1. Reusing an identifier that is still in        │
│  flight would require `next_free_mid` to wrap after ~65534 allocations. It   │
│  is recorded here only as the latent side effect that the fix removes, not   │
│  as a separate vulnerability.                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Start the `sol` broker on a loopback port from the ASan/UBSan build      │
│  (`build-asan/sol`) or the plain optimized build (`build-dev/sol`).          │
│  2. Open a TCP connection and send the CONNECT packet (`10 19 00 04 4d 51    │
│  54 54 04 02 00 3c 00 0d 70 6c 76 2d 61 63 6b 2d 70 72 6f 62 65`); the       │
│  broker answers `20 00 00`.                                                  │
│  3. Send the four bytes `40 02 00 01` (PUBACK, Remaining Length 2, packet    │
│  identifier 1, which the broker never allocated).                            │
│  4. Observe the connection close with no reply, and the broker process       │
│  terminate: the sanitizer build reports `member access within null pointer   │
│  of type 'struct mqtt_packet'` at the `inflight_msg_clear` call in           │
│  `puback_handler` (`src/handlers.c:793`); the plain build dies with signal   │
│  11.                                                                         │
│  5. Repeat with `70 02 00 01` (PUBCOMP) — same result at                     │
│  `src/handlers.c:848`. Repeat with `40 02 ff ff` / `70 02 ff ff` — same,     │
│  showing the identifier is in range and the problem is the NULL slot.        │
│  6. Repeat step 3 without any CONNECT: the session pointer itself is NULL    │
│  and the crash is identical, so no credentials or prior handshake are        │
│  needed.                                                                     │
│  7. Control: perform a real QoS 1 round trip (SUBSCRIBE at QoS 1, publish    │
│  from a second connection, receive the QoS 1 PUBLISH with its mid, then      │
│  PUBACK that mid) — the same four bytes are accepted, PINGREQ afterwards is  │
│  answered `d0`, and the broker stays alive.                                  │
│  8. Duplicate case: after the legal PUBACK in step 7, send the same `40 02   │
│  00 01` again — the broker terminates with signal 11, with no attacker       │
│  input beyond the client retrying its own acknowledgement.                   │
│  9. Apply the patch, rebuild, and repeat steps 3, 5, 6 and 8: no crash, the  │
│  spurious acks are ignored, and the legitimate round trip in step 7 still    │
│  works.                                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
