│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Treat every acknowledgement as untrusted input and validate ownership       │
│  before touching the in-flight table. Add a single helper that returns the   │
│  in-flight slot for a packet identifier only when the client actually has a  │
│  session, the identifier is inside the table, and the slot holds a live      │
│  packet; have the PUBACK and PUBCOMP handlers call it under the client       │
│  mutex and, when it returns nothing, log and ignore the acknowledgement      │
│  instead of dereferencing and decrementing it. Decrement the in-flight       │
│  counter only when a live slot was released, so the counter cannot wrap.     │
│  Apply the same ownership rule to any future handler that resolves a         │
│  client-supplied packet identifier into session state (for example           │
│  PUBREC/PUBREL bookkeeping), and consider rejecting ACK packets outright on  │
│  connections that have not completed CONNECT, so session state is never      │
│  reached before authentication.                                              │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0005 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Non-terminating SUBSCRIBE/UNSUBSCRIBE tuple loops: unsigned          │
│  underflow lets one 7-byte pre-auth packet crash or exhaust the broker       │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: sol MQTT broker, source snapshot (repository /workspace/source)     │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: MQTT SUBSCRIBE (0x82) and UNSUBSCRIBE (0xA2) packet decoders      │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: TCP                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  Both the SUBSCRIBE and the UNSUBSCRIBE decoders walk their payload in a     │
│  loop whose counter is the sender-declared packet length, and they subtract  │
│  the declared topic length from it before checking that those bytes are      │
│  actually present. Declaring a topic longer than the packet wraps the        │
│  counter to about 2^64, so the loop never terminates: it keeps reallocating  │
│  and allocating memory and keeps copying from beyond the end of the          │
│  connection's read buffer. A single unauthenticated 7-byte packet kills the  │
│  shipped build with SIGSEGV in about 0.2 s; with a larger read buffer it     │
│  instead grows resident memory by roughly 900 MB in half a second while      │
│  pinning a worker thread at full CPU, and the process no longer honours      │
│  SIGTERM.                                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An unauthenticated network peer needs one TCP connection and 7 bytes - no   │
│  CONNECT, no credentials - to take the broker down. On a build with the      │
│  project's production flags the whole process dies with SIGSEGV within a     │
│  fraction of a second (denial of service for every connected client). With   │
│  the shipped 512 KB / 1 MB read-buffer configuration, and on builds where    │
│  the allocator keeps up, the same packet instead produces an unbounded       │
│  loop: resident memory grew from 52 MB to 965 MB within 0.5 s while one of   │
│  the two event-loop threads stayed at ~95 % CPU, the connection stayed       │
│  open, no diagnostic was produced, and the process ignored SIGTERM. In that  │
│  state roughly half of the broker's event-loop capacity is lost permanently  │
│  per malicious connection, and further connections exhaust memory until the  │
│  process is killed. The same loop copies topic bytes from beyond the end of  │
│  the read buffer, so callers that do not crash first perform unbounded       │
│  out-of-bounds heap reads.                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The two decoders in `src/mqtt.c` consume the variable header and payload    │
│  with a loop counter (`len`, a `usize`) that is the sender-declared          │
│  Remaining Length. Every subtraction happens before the corresponding        │
│  availability test, and the declared topic length is taken straight from     │
│  the wire:                                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/mqtt.c startLineNumber=341 endLineNumber=358                 │
│      usize i = 0;                                                            │
│      for (; len > 0; ++i) {                                                  │
│          /* Read length bytes of the first topic filter */                   │
│          len -= sizeof(u16);                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│          /* We have to make room for additional incoming tuples */           │
│          subscribe.tuples =                                                  │
│              try_realloc(subscribe.tuples, (i + 1) *                         │
│  sizeof(*subscribe.tuples));                                                 │
│          subscribe.tuples[i].topic_len =                                     │
│              unpack_string16(&buf, &subscribe.tuples[i].topic);              │
│                                                                              │
│          if (!subscribe.tuples[i].topic)                                     │
│              goto err;                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│          len -= subscribe.tuples[i].topic_len;                               │
│          subscribe.tuples[i].qos = unpack_integer(&buf, 'B');                │
│          len -= sizeof(u8);                                                  │
│      }                                                                       │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `unpack_mqtt_unsubscribe` (`src/mqtt.c:386-401`) has the identical shape    │
│  with a 2-tuple. `topic_len` is the u16 that `unpack_string16` has just      │
│  read from the packet (`src/pack.c:420-425`), so the value subtracted at     │
│  `src/mqtt.c:355` / `:400` is entirely attacker-controlled and is never      │
│  compared with what is left of the packet.                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  With the 7-byte SUBSCRIBE `82 05 00 01 00 FF 41` the pre-loop bookkeeping   │
│  leaves `len = 1` (`Remaining Length 5`, minus the 2-byte packet             │
│  identifier, minus the 2-byte topic length). The declared topic length is    │
│  `0x00FF = 255`, so `len = 1 - 255 = 18446744073709551362`, and `len > 0`    │
│  remains true for ~2^64 iterations. The same packet with opcode `0xA2`       │
│  reaches the identical arithmetic in `unpack_mqtt_unsubscribe`.              │
│                                                                              │
│  Each iteration of the resulting loop has three effects:                     │
│                                                                              │
│  1. `try_realloc(subscribe.tuples, (i + 1) * sizeof(*subscribe.tuples))`     │
│  grows the tuple array by 16 bytes per iteration, without bound and without  │
│  regard to `max_memory`.                                                     │
│  2. `unpack_string16` allocates `topic_len + 1` bytes and copies             │
│  `topic_len` bytes from `buf`, which advances by `2 + topic_len` (+1 for     │
│  the QoS byte) per iteration, so the copy source leaves the per-client read  │
│  buffer (`client->rbuf`, allocated with exactly `max_request_size` bytes at  │
│  `src/server.c:392-394`) after about `max_request_size / 3` iterations and   │
│  keeps walking the heap.                                                     │
│  3. Nothing is ever freed on this path: the tuple array and every topic      │
│  string stay live, so resident memory grows by roughly `topic_len + 16`      │
│  bytes per iteration.                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  The only exit from the loop is `if (!subscribe.tuples[i].topic) goto        │
│  err;`, which is dead code because `unpack_bytes` (`src/pack.c:411-418`)     │
│  calls `exit(EXIT_FAILURE)` on allocation failure instead of returning       │
│  NULL.                                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Two smaller weaknesses in the same arithmetic: `src/mqtt.c:332` / `:377`    │
│  subtract the 2-byte packet identifier before any check that `len >= 2`,     │
│  and `subscribe.tuples_len = i` truncates an unbounded iteration count into  │
│  a u16.                                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  The pattern is present in the initial commit of the file (`git blame        │
│  src/mqtt.c -L 342,357` attributes every line to `373d848`, 2024-12-12) and  │
│  no later change bounded the loop.                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Control analysis: `recv_packet` limits only the *total* advertised packet   │
│  to `max_request_size` (`src/server.c:551`); it never validates a field      │
│  length against the bytes present. `unpack_string16` does not compare its    │
│  declared length with the remaining packet length. Decoding happens in       │
│  `process_message` (`src/server.c:865`) before any CONNECT or credential     │
│  check, so the defect is reachable pre-authentication regardless of the      │
│  `allow_anonymous` / `password_file` configuration.                          │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Build the broker from the snapshot twice: once with the project's        │
│  production flags and once with AddressSanitizer/UBSan instrumentation for   │
│  the memory-access view.                                                     │
│  2. Start an instance bound to 127.0.0.1 with `max_request_size 1MB` (the    │
│  shipped-style configuration), and a second instance with `max_request_size  │
│  4096` so the read buffer is small.                                          │
│  3. Open one TCP connection to the listening port and send the seven bytes   │
│  `82 05 00 01 00 FF 41`. Do not send a CONNECT packet and do not             │
│  authenticate.                                                               │
│  4. Against the production-flags instance, observe the process disappear     │
│  within ~0.2 s (client sees EOF, exit status -11/SIGSEGV); a core dump       │
│  shows the fault in `unpack_integer` reached from `unpack_string16` inside   │
│  `unpack_mqtt_subscribe` with `len = 18446744073707446302`.                  │
│  5. Repeat step 3 with the seven bytes `A2 05 00 01 00 FF 41`; the fault     │
│  moves one call site over, into `unpack_mqtt_unsubscribe` with the same      │
│  wrapped `len`.                                                              │
│  6. Against the 4 KiB Read-buffer instance, observe the AddressSanitizer     │
│  report `heap-buffer-overflow READ of size 1` at `src/pack.c:91`             │
│  (`unpacku16`) reached from `unpack_mqtt_subscribe` at `src/mqtt.c:350`,     │
│  about 0.2 s after the packet is sent - the loop has walked off the read     │
│  buffer.                                                                     │
│  7. Against the 1 MB instance, sample `/proc/<pid>/status` and               │
│  `/proc/<pid>/stat` for six seconds: resident memory climbs from ~52 MB to   │
│  ~950 MB within the first 0.5 s while the process accumulates CPU time on    │
│  one thread, no sanitizer report is produced, the connection stays open,     │
│  and SIGTERM is not honoured within four seconds.                            │
│  8. Confirm the packet is not merely a publishing-without-CONNECT artefact:  │
│  the same broker keeps serving other clients normally until the looping      │
│  packet arrives.                                                             │
│                                                                              │
