╭─ VULN-0006 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Pre-auth heap out-of-bounds write from unbounded Remaining-Length    │
│  decode and unchecked toread in recv_packet                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: sol MQTT broker, source snapshot (repository /workspace/source)     │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: MQTT fixed-header / Remaining-Length framing (recv_packet)        │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: TCP                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The packet-framing code decodes the variable-length Remaining Length field  │
│  without the four-byte limit the MQTT standard sets, and it does so on       │
│  bytes it has not received, so continuation bits in stale read-buffer        │
│  content keep the decode walking. The number of bytes consumed that way is   │
│  then added to the attacker's declared length to decide how much more to     │
│  read from the socket, without checking that total against the actual size   │
│  of the per-client read buffer. The socket read therefore writes past the    │
│  end of the buffer with bytes and a length the attacker chooses; sanitizer   │
│  instrumentation reports heap-buffer-overflow writes of up to ~4 KB past a   │
│  64 KiB buffer and a decode walk that reads off the end of the allocation.   │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An unauthenticated peer can make the broker write attacker-chosen bytes     │
│  beyond the end of the per-client read buffer, which is a heap-corruption    │
│  primitive on the request-framing path - the first code that runs for any    │
│  connection, before CONNECT or authentication. The write length is derived   │
│  from how far the attacker can drive the length-field walk into stale        │
│  buffer content, so on the shipped 512 KB / 1 MB buffers the write can       │
│  reach up to roughly a megabyte past the allocation; the demonstrated case   │
│  writes 4001 bytes past a 64 KiB buffer (reported by AddressSanitizer as a   │
│  69533-byte out-of-bounds write into the heap). The same defect produces an  │
│  out-of-bounds heap read of arbitrary distance (the decoder reads until it   │
│  finds a byte without the continuation bit) and a framing desynchronisation  │
│  where a client is left waiting on a packet the broker never finishes        │
│  reading. What is proven is the write primitive, the out-of-bounds read and  │
│  a sanitizer-detected process abort; conversion into controlled code         │
│  execution was not demonstrated, and on the uninstrumented build the single  │
│  overrun I tested did not by itself crash the process.                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  Two defects combine in the framing path.                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  **1. The length decode has no bound and reads bytes that were never         │
│  received.** `src/mqtt.c:136-153`:                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/mqtt.c startLineNumber=136 endLineNumber=153                 │
│  usize mqtt_decode_length(u8 *buf, unsigned *pos)                            │
│  {                                                                           │
│      u8 c;                                                                   │
│      u64 multiplier = 1LL;                                                   │
│      u64 value      = 0LL;                                                   │
│      *pos           = 0;                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│      do {                                                                    │
│          c = *buf;                                                           │
│          value += (c & 127) * multiplier;                                    │
│          multiplier *= 128;                                                  │
│          ++buf;                                                              │
│          ++(*pos);                                                           │
│      } while ((c & 128) != 0);                                               │
│                                                                              │
│      return value;                                                           │
│  }                                                                           │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  The loop stops only on a byte whose bit 7 is clear. `MAX_LEN_BYTES`         │
│  (defined at `src/mqtt.c:59`) is used exclusively by the encoder             │
│  (`mqtt_encode_length`, `src/mqtt.c:112`); the decoder has no equivalent     │
│  cap, and the comment above it records the gap as a TODO ("Handle case       │
│  where multiplier > 128 * 128 * 128").                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Its caller violates the decoder's implicit precondition. `recv_packet`      │
│  reads at most four bytes into `c->rbuf` (`src/server.c:484` for the first   │
│  two, `src/server.c:530` for two more) and then calls the decoder on         │
│  `c->rbuf + 1`, i.e. on `rbuf[1], rbuf[2], rbuf[3]` - three bytes that were  │
│  received and `rbuf[4..]`, which were not. `rbuf` is not cleared between     │
│  packets (`process_message` resets only `toread`, `read` and `rpos`), and    │
│  `client_init` reuses a `struct client` from a pool without touching an      │
│  already allocated `rbuf` (`src/server.c:392-394`), so `rbuf[4..]` holds     │
│  either the previous packet's bytes on the same connection or bytes primed   │
│  by an earlier connection that occupied the same pool slot. An attacker who  │
│  first sends a normally framed packet whose body is a run of `0x80` bytes    │
│  chooses that content.                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  **2. `toread` is computed from the decoded length and the walk length, but  │
│  only the decoded length is validated.** `src/server.c:545-575`:             │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/server.c startLineNumber=545 endLineNumber=575               │
│          pktlen = mqtt_decode_length(c->rbuf + 1, &pos);                     │
│                                                                              │
│          /*                                                                  │
│           * Set return code to -ERRMAXREQSIZE in case the total packet len   │
│           * exceeds the configuration limit `max_request_size`               │
│           */                                                                 │
│          if (pktlen > conf->max_request_size)                                │
│              return -ERRMAXREQSIZE;                                          │
│                                                                              │
│          /*                                                                  │
│           * Update the toread field for the client with the entire length    │
│  of                                                                          │
│           * the current packet, which is comprehensive of packet length,     │
│           * bytes used to encode it and 1 byte for the header                │
│           */                                                                 │
│          c->rpos   = pos + 1;                                                │
│          c->toread = pktlen + pos + 1; // pos = bytes used to store length   │
│                                                                              │
│          /* Looks like we got an ACK packet, we're done reading */           │
│          if (pktlen <= 4)                                                    │
│              goto exit;                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│          c->status = WAITING_DATA;                                           │
│      }                                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│      nread = recv_data(&c->conn, c->rbuf + c->read, c->toread - c->read);    │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `c->rbuf` is allocated with exactly `conf->max_request_size` bytes          │
│  (`src/server.c:392-394`, default `512KB` per `src/config.h:73`). The check  │
│  on line 551 bounds `pktlen` only, while `toread = pktlen + pos + 1`         │
│  includes the fixed header byte and the `pos` length bytes, and `pos` is     │
│  whatever the unbound walk consumed - attacker-influenced through the stale  │
│  content. Whenever `pktlen == max_request_size` (the comparison is strict    │
│  `>`), `toread` exceeds the allocation by `pos + 1` bytes and the `recv` on  │
│  line 575 writes that far past the end of the buffer. The observed           │
│  arithmetic for a 64 KiB buffer is `pktlen = 65536`, `pos = 4000`, `toread   │
│  = 69537`: a 4001-byte overwrite of whatever follows `rbuf` on the heap,     │
│  carrying the attacker's socket bytes.                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Two further details in the same code: `pktlen` is declared `unsigned int`   │
│  while `mqtt_decode_length` returns `usize`, so a five-or-more-byte length   │
│  field truncates the decoded value (this is reachable from the stale-walk    │
│  primitive as well and is why the fix moves the variable to `usize`), and    │
│  `pos` can grow to the length of the stale run, i.e. up to the size of the   │
│  read buffer itself.                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Runtime confirmation on the uninstrumented build (measured under the        │
│  debugger at the two source lines above):                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  ```                                                                         │
│  BEFORE-DECODE: c->read=4 c->rpos=0 c->toread=0                              │
│  AT-RECV-575: c->toread=33102 c->read=4 rbuf_capacity=65536  (toread - read  │
│  = 33098)   <- priming packet                                                │
│  AT-RECV-575: c->toread=69537 c->read=4 rbuf_capacity=65536  (toread - read  │
│  = 69533)   <- trigger                                                       │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Controls: none. The decoder cannot tell received bytes from stale ones,     │
│  there is no cap on continuation bytes, and no code path re-checks `toread`  │
│  against the buffer size. `max_request_size` therefore does not protect the  │
│  read buffer at all. Framing runs inside `recv_packet` before any CONNECT    │
│  or credential check, so the primitive is reachable pre-authentication with  │
│  a bare TCP connection.                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Build the broker from the snapshot with AddressSanitizer/UBSan           │
│  instrumentation, and separately with the project's production flags.        │
│  2. Start an instance bound to 127.0.0.1 with `max_request_size 65536`.      │
│  3. Open one TCP connection. First send a normally framed PUBLISH (`0x30`,   │
│  a well-formed Remaining Length, a 2-byte topic length of `0x8080` and an    │
│  all-`0x80` body) that fits the configured limit. This leaves the read       │
│  buffer filled with continuation bytes.                                      │
│  4. On the same connection, send the four bytes `30 80 80 84` followed by    │
│  69533 bytes. The 4th length byte is out of the received window, so the      │
│  decoder continues into the primed buffer until the byte at offset 4000      │
│  terminates the walk, giving `pos = 4000` and `toread = 65536 + 4000 + 1 =   │
│  69537` on a 65536-byte buffer.                                              │
│  5. Observe the sanitizer report: `heap-buffer-overflow ... WRITE of size    │
│  69533` with the stack `recv <- recv_bytes <- conn_recv <- recv_data <-      │
│  recv_packet (src/server.c:575)`, destination one byte past the end of the   │
│  read-buffer allocation.                                                     │
│  6. To observe the out-of-bounds read on its own, prime the read buffer      │
│  with continuation bytes that extend to its end and then send `30 80 80      │
│  80`: the report is `heap-buffer-overflow ... READ of size 1` inside         │
│  `mqtt_decode_length (src/mqtt.c:145)` from `recv_packet                     │
│  (src/server.c:545)`.                                                        │
│  7. To observe the same effect with a single frame and no priming, use a 1   │
│  MiB limit and send `30 80 80 C0` followed by 1048576 bytes: the declared    │
│  length equals the limit and passes the size check, while `pos = 4` makes    │
│  `toread = 1048581` against a 1048576-byte buffer.                           │
│  8. Confirm reachability without authentication by noting that no CONNECT    │
│  packet is sent in any step, and that after the broker rejects the frame it  │
│  continues to answer PINGREQ on a fresh connection.                          │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python title=poc_framing.py                                              │
│  #!/usr/bin/env python3                                                      │
│  """PoC: unbounded Remaining-Length decode and unchecked `toread`            │
│  (framing).                                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  usage: SOL_BIN=/path/to/sol python3 poc_framing.py                          │
│  {toread|prime-walk|toread-mult} [max_request_size]                          │
│  """                                                                         │
│  import os                                                                   │
│  import shutil                                                               │
│  import signal                                                               │
│  import socket                                                               │
│  import subprocess                                                           │
│  import sys                                                                  │
│  import time                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  BIN = os.environ.get("SOL_BIN", "/usr/local/bin/sol")                       │
│  PORT = int(os.environ.get("SOL_PORT", "19600"))                             │
│  MAXREQ_TEXT = sys.argv[2] if len(sys.argv) > 2 else "1MB"                   │
│  DIR = "/tmp/sol-poc-framing"                                                │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  def parse_size(text):                                                       │
│      text = text.strip().upper()                                             │
